#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106748} :activity/id #uuid "58252d2b-905f-529d-99aa-1e8c7d226207" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050785 :topic/id #uuid "66e72e13-4959-5704-8bb3-c0ffea916dd8" :topic/math-academy-id 1384} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107031 :tutorial/content "Rational functions have infinite limits when the $x$-value approaches vertical asymptotes. To find the infinite limits of a rational function $f(x)$, we first find the vertical asymptotes.\n\nFor example, consider the rational function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-2}\n$$\n\nThis rational function has a vertical asymptote at $x=2$.\n\nTo find the one-sided limits at the asymptote $x=2$, we check the sign of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and the right.\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^-$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $1.9$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-10$ | $-100$ | $-1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are negative, and they get more and more negative. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^+$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the right:\n\n| $x$ | $2.1$ | $2.01$ | $2.001$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $10$ | $100$ | $1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are positive, and they get larger and larger. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = +\\infty\n$$\n\nThese one-sided limits match up with what we see in the graph of $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/24/248e651214f9339d5e47f09f3eecd3387897eda525d99845258d4466025f16ac.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c5a2dd1f-90e1-5bcc-a854-1debabaffe1b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1338 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5567f868-361e-566c-80a1-f886af15f9ca" :step/math-academy-id 2651 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106749 :step/c